    "description": "HEY! WE'RE TEAM GRANOLA \ud83d\udc4b\n\nWe're building a category-defining product, backed by a $125M Series C and used daily by teams at companies like Cursor, Vanta, and Linear. We started by making meeting notes better. Now we're turning every conversation a company has into context that people and agents can act on: shared team workspaces, APIs and integrations, and agentic workflows that do something with what was said.\n\nIf you haven't already, you should check out what we're building https://www.granola.ai, and why you should work here https://www.granola.ai/jobs.\n\nThe Granola team is kind, curious and design-obsessed. Many of us are former founders, others have built products at Figma, Apple, Google and Meta, and we're all still learning from each other.\n\nYou'll own the systems that make Granola fast, accurate and trustworthy for the next 100x of users, and you'll help decide what's worth building. Our backend engineers talk to users and ship product, not just services.\n\nWHAT YOU'LL WORK ON\n\n- The voice pipeline. Scale real-time transcription so notes land accurately in seconds, on every device people use.\n\n- New models in production. Evaluate new voice and transcription models as they're released, and get the best ones into users' hands.\n\n- The agent harness. Build the harness Granola's agents run in: long-running jobs, tool calls, retries, and retrieving the right context.\n\n- APIs and integrations. Extend the APIs and integrations that let people bring Granola context into the AI tools they already use.\n\n- Enterprise-grade trust. Build the permissions, data isolation and admin controls that let a company roll Granola out across every team.\n\n- Data at scale. Own Postgres performance, observability and alerting as usage grows.\n\nWHO WE'RE LOOKING FOR\n\nWe don't set a minimum number of years. Some of our strongest engineers graduated two years ago; others have run their own companies. We care about what you've built and how you think about it.\n\n- You've owned backend systems in production that real users depended on, and you can talk through the trade-offs you made.\n\n- You're strong in TypeScript and Node.js.\n\n- You know relational databases deeply (we use Postgres): query plans, indexing, and migrations without downtime.\n\n- You're comfortable with cloud infrastructure as code (we use AWS, Terraform and a lot of Lambda).\n\n- You think about security and failure modes ",
