    "description": "ABOUT SUPABASE\n\nSupabase is the Postgres development platform, built by developers for developers. We provide a complete backend solution including Database, Auth, Storage, Edge Functions, Realtime, and Vector Search. All services are deeply integrated and designed for growth.\n\nABOUT THE ROLE\n\nWe're looking for a SDK Engineer - Python to join our SDK team and own how developers talk to Supabase from Python. You'll work closely with the rest of the SDK team, our Auth, Realtime, Storage, PostgREST, Functions, CLI and Docs teams, and the open-source community, helping us make the Python SDK a library Python developers genuinely enjoy using.\n\nPython developers reach Supabase from backend services, data pipelines, notebooks, and a growing number of AI agents and agent frameworks. The Python SDK is how all of them talk to the platform: database queries, auth, storage, realtime and edge functions, in both sync and async flavors.\n\nThis is a library-authoring role, and the work happens in the open: public repos, public issue trackers, and API decisions whose consequences last for years. If you get satisfaction from a type checker that catches the bug before your users do, from an async client that cleans up after itself, and from an issue tracker that isn't a graveyard, you'll like it here.\n\nThis role is ideal for someone who thrives in async, fast-paced environments and is excited about building developer tools that scale to millions.\n\nWHAT YOU'LL BE RESPONSIBLE FOR\n\n- Build and evolve our Python SDK across database, auth, storage, realtime and functions, with both sync and async APIs that feel natural in Python.\n\n- Start new things. Alongside the maintenance work, find what's missing, make the case for it, and build it. Nobody will hand you the list.\n\n- Work in the open. Triage inbound issues, review and shepherd outside contributions, keep CI and release automation healthy, and hold a clear, kind line on scope.\n\n- Own the typing experience. Make the SDK fully typed and checker-friendly, so autocomplete and static analysis stay correct in real codebases.\n\n- Keep us honest across environments. Multiple Python versions, sync and async frameworks, workers, serverless and notebooks \u2014 with dependency ranges that don't fight our users' projects. Test it, don't assume it.\n\n- Work on realtime. Long-lived websocket connections, reconnection, and subscription and presence se",
