    "description": "Before the detail, here's the challenge you'd help us solve.\n\nWe build the embodied intelligence that moves real vehicles safely, and the ecosystem a billion machines will run on in the future. Very few people in AI can say this. Every role here, whatever the team, plugs into that.\n\nHere\u2019s what this particular role covers.\n\n\ud83d\udee0\ufe0f ABOUT OUR ENGINEERING TEAMS\n\nWayve\u2019s engineering teams are building the AI, robotics, simulation, data, and systems foundations needed to deploy a generalisable AI Driver safely and at scale.\n\nABOUT THE VEHICLE GENERALIZATION TEAM\n\nVehicle Generalization makes it efficient, scalable, and predictable to deploy Wayve\u2019s autonomous driving stack across a wide range of vehicle platforms by making our core autonomy generalise as widely as possible.\n\nWe work across pre- and post-training to improve how well our AI models generalise zero-shot to changes in perception\u2014including cameras, intrinsics, field of view, lens distortion, and calibration\u2014and embodiment, including vehicle dynamics, actuation limits, and rig geometry. We also own the Generalization Benchmark, Wayve\u2019s tool for measuring how models transfer across platforms. Our work goes into production programmes with major OEMs.\n\nWe\u2019re looking for a hands-on Machine Learning Engineer with a strong robotics background who understands how a real vehicle senses, estimates its state, and moves. You\u2019ll identify where our models break down across platforms, understand why, and address those failures in training\u2014then prove the improvement through rigorous evaluation.\n\n\ud83e\udde0 YOUR DAY-TO-DAY\n\n- Design and train models that are robust to differences between vehicles, using approaches such as conditioning or augmentation for vehicle kinematics and camera parameters.\n\n- Create benchmarks and metrics for cross-platform performance, translating results into clear decisions for model and platform teams.\n\n- Help root-cause on-road issues such as late turns, lane-keeping, and steering problems. Work with controls, calibration, and OEM engineers to separate model problems from system problems and identify the highest-impact model improvements.\n\n- Move research into production in collaboration with Driving Core and application engineering teams.\n\n- Support customer programmes, with some travel.\n\n- Mentor teammates and help raise the bar for ML engineering across the team.\n\n\ud83e\udde9 WHAT YOU\u2019LL BE WORKING ON\n\n- Pre- a",
